light-neutrino-mass-from-type-i-seesaw-1.interpretation:
This formula is a scalar approximation derived from the diagonalization of the full neutrino mass matrix. The term $m_{
u}$ represents the mass of the light mass eigenstate, which is predominantly the observed active left-handed neutrino. The term $m_D$ is the Dirac mass, arising from the Yukawa coupling of the neutrino to the Higgs field, and is typically assumed to be of the same order as other charged lepton or quark masses. The crucial term $M_R$ is the large Majorana mass of the hypothetical sterile, right-handed neutrino, which is a singlet under the Standard Model gauge group. The inverse relationship, $m_{
u} \propto 1/M_R$, is the 'seesaw' itself: a very large $M_R$ (e.g., near the Grand Unification scale, $\sim 10^{15}$ GeV) naturally suppresses $m_{
u}$ to the observed sub-eV scale. The negative sign is a consequence of the block diagonalization of the mass matrix, specifically from the term $-M_D M_R^{-1} M_D^T$, and is often absorbed when only considering the magnitude of the mass.
